+++ b/research/domstring-input.md
@@ -0,0 +1,79 @@
+# Finite DOMString code-unit input
+
+`DomString.decode` interprets a finite list of 16-bit code units as code points.
+Adjacent leading/trailing surrogate pairs combine into their supplementary code
+point; unmatched surrogates retain their numeric value. `DomString.preprocess`
+then applies `CodePointInput.preprocess`, which replaces those remaining
+surrogates and performs CSS CRLF/CR/FF/NUL normalization. Existing scalar and
+code-point entry points are unchanged.
+
+`CssDomStringInput` provides the executable adapter and independent `Step` and
+`Decoded` relations. `CssDomStringInputLaws` proves exact code point/suffix
+correspondence, one/two-unit consumption, finite decoding correspondence and
+uniqueness, length bounds and composition with the independent CSS filtering
+relation. `CssDomStringInputExamples` registers 34 ordered observations, a
+kernel-checked aggregate theorem and three additional anonymous examples.
+All 28 named theorems in the four-module package are in the standard audit.
+
+`CssDomStringScalarChoice` proves that replacing surrogate code points with
+U+FFFD before CSS filtering leaves the final filtered result unchanged. This
+filtering-boundary equality does not establish equivalence of all DOMString and
+USVString operations, stored source, serialization, indexing or exceptions.
+It does not model a scalar-value string's re-encoding into code units. A wrong
+early replacement is observable at the adapter output even though filtering can
+hide it, so adapter-level examples matter.
+
+## Specification mapping and caller qualification
+
+WebIDL maps DOMString to Infra strings and preserves the units of the successful
+JavaScript `ToString` result. Infra strings contain 16-bit units; when interpreted
+as code points, they follow ECMAScript's String Type rules. Those rules combine
+valid pairs while preserving unmatched surrogates. The Lean source is a manually
+authored interpretation, not a mechanically verified translation of that prose.
+Its range predicates are shared by the program and relation: internal agreement
+alone does not prove their values match the specification.
+
+CSSOM permits each implementation to define `CSSOMString` as either DOMString
+or USVString. `CSSStyleSheet.insertRule` takes CSSOMString and calls CSS Syntax's
+parse-rule algorithm after the origin-clean and disallow-modification guards.
+Parse-rule normalizes its input; the string branch filters code points before
+tokenizing. Thus the DOMString route is conditional on that type choice,
+successful coercion and the pre-parse guards. `replace` and `replaceSync`
+explicitly take USVString and are not DOMString examples. No Firefox type choice,
+CSSOM state machine or end-to-end browser behavior is proved here.
+
+The primary sources, acquired on 2026-11-10, are pinned in `coverage.json`:
+
+| Source | Immutable version | Full-file SHA-256 |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| WebIDL types and conversion | [0d862c6573d0ecca327b7ba00210e7c051c988b3](https://github.com/whatwg/webidl/blob/0d862c6573d0ecca327b7ba00210e7c051c988b3/index.bs) | `b467604348aae390a9734cf639bb77a2505cf88748287831768fc222240cf3e4` |
+| Infra strings | [c67ae1fb1d161b23d0f8c9bf9917ea1993f7b19a](https://github.com/whatwg/infra/blob/c67ae1fb1d161b23d0f8c9bf9917ea1993f7b19a/infra.bs) | `687e65669b9d85c2147a139513fe319097cf84af1005f5c9d7cb3d2ade0c1aed` |
+| ECMAScript String Type, CodePointAt, StringToCodePoints | [828d35d00f8584c4a0fbcac7742b57c7b959c3f3](https://github.com/tc39/ecma262/blob/828d35d00f8584c4a0fbcac7742b57c7b959c3f3/spec.html) | `f977d170323cfeacaae9086310f46df500a58c3d463ab955d511543b9f64c51c` |
+| CSSOM caller/type choice | [5823c7a7c3401c6b434dbf3ef88077ea45e30303](https://github.com/w3c/csswg-drafts/blob/5823c7a7c3401c6b434dbf3ef88077ea45e30303/cssom-1/Overview.bs) | `a969093ae15f352b7136e2eb097bacbb7a574480a914547484ce61909592e876` |
+| CSS Syntax input filtering and parser entry | [c410ee5e4d2afe6d8b2e8ce53723b8d1eb3b5b67](https://github.com/w3c/csswg-drafts/blob/c410ee5e4d2afe6d8b2e8ce53723b8d1eb3b5b67/css-syntax-3/Overview.bs) | `4a4a5a3994bda10a56885e66776169d0e05d5a7515b0def4e8859c20e3fd4afc` |
+
+## Domain and limits
+
+Input is `List (Fin 0x10000)`. Numeric callers should use `unitOfNat?`,
+`decodeValues`, `prepareValues` or `nextValues`; direct numeric `Fin` literals
+can wrap. Out-of-range numbers are admission failures, not CSS parse errors.
+`nextValues` distinguishes invalid input (`none`) from admitted empty input
+(`some none`). Supplementary code-point numbers are not valid individual units.
+
+The model covers arbitrary finite list lengths. `JavaScriptLength` separately
+states ECMAScript's 2^53−1 maximum; numeric admission does not enforce it. The
+theorems therefore cover a mathematical superset of actual JavaScript strings.
+There is no byte decoder, endianness/BOM policy, DOM acquisition, general
+ToString/coercion behavior, Unicode normalization, arbitrary-index CodePointAt
+wrapper or IsUnpairedSurrogate return field. No streaming or chunk-boundary
+behavior is proved: splitting a valid pair across chunks changes stateless
+decoding, and CSS filtering additionally has a CRLF boundary.
+
+## Validation scope
+
+`python3 check.py` includes this package in the full build, executable vector and
+axiom audit. `python3 check.py --coverage-only` checks static registrations only.
+The integration used clean sources for the ten-module input dependency closure:
+70 theorem audits and 79 observations (34 new, 21 code-point and 24 scalar), plus
+global static registrations and deliberate integration controls. It does not
+refresh a whole-library proof receipt or any native/browser result.
